## Anti-patterns

| Don't | Why |
|---|---|
| Paste hex codes and spacing into `/speckit.specify` | The spec template strips implementation detail; the values get lost or mutated. They belong in `design-context.md`. |
| One spec for the whole application | One `/speckit.specify` = one branch = one folder. Giant specs produce giant unreviewable task lists. |
| Skip feature 001 (shell + design system) | Every subsequent screen then invents its own near-duplicate components. This is the main source of visual drift. |
| Rely on the live MCP session as your record | Sessions end. `design/` is the durable artifact; the MCP is how you populate it. |
| Put Snowflake table names anywhere in this repo | They will leak into UI assumptions. Your contract is `openapi.yaml` and nothing behind it. |
| Let the agent compute a financial figure client-side | Constitution principle 2. Two sources of truth for a number is a production incident waiting. |
| Fix drift by re-prompting the agent | Fix `constitution.md` or `design-context.md` and regenerate. Prompt nudges don't persist. |
| Float on `main` for the CLI | Command names and flags have changed repeatedly. Pin the release tag. |

---

## Known limitation, stated plainly

Design fidelity through Spec Kit is not solved. A documented case in the Spec Kit discussions
describes a user with Figma dev-mode MCP connected and all design details — colours,
typography, spacing, radii — captured in `tasks.md`, whose implemented UI still did not match
Figma. Fidelity leaks between task definition and code generation.

The four mitigations in this runbook, in order of effect:

1. **Token-only constitution rule.** If a component physically cannot contain a literal value,
   the model cannot drift into approximations.
2. **Feature 001 first**, so later screens compose rather than invent.
3. **Code Connect**, binding Figma components to real import paths.
4. **`checklists/visual.md` as a human gate** before merge.

Expect roughly 90–95% fidelity out of the flow and a manual polish pass on spacing and hover
states. That is a good outcome, and it is honest to plan for it rather than promise
pixel-perfection.
























# Spec-Driven Development with GitHub Spec Kit
### A working playbook for WorkplaceIQ

> **Deck source file.** Each `##` heading is intended to become one slide (a few are marked "split across 2 slides" where content is dense). Speaker notes are in `> Note:` blocks.

---

## Agenda

1. Where we are today — greenfield, high ambiguity, AI-assisted coding
2. What Spec-Driven Development (SDD) actually is
3. Why SDD fits WorkplaceIQ specifically
4. Spec Kit: install, artifacts, and the command set
5. The workflow, command by command — with real WorkplaceIQ prompts
6. Live demo: one feature, spec → running code
7. How we adopt it: ways of working, DoD, review gates
8. Anti-patterns, objections, and answers
9. Cheat sheet

> Note: Target ~35 min talk + 15 min demo. The demo is the persuasion; the slides are the reference material people come back to.

---

## The situation we are in

**WorkplaceIQ, in one line:** ingest Shareworks and E*TRADE stock plan data into Snowflake under a unified model, run analytics on it, and surface insight to company (plan sponsor) admins so they can make data-based decisions — showing how equity plan solutions drive employee retention and how employees actually use their equity.

**Our team's slice:** the UI and the Spring Boot service that talks to Snowflake.

What makes this hard:

- **Two source systems, two vocabularies.** Shareworks and E*TRADE do not agree on what a "grant", "tranche", "award type", or "participant" is. The unified model *is* the product risk.
- **Greenfield.** No existing code to read for intent. There is no "look at how the old service did it."
- **Regulated, sponsor-facing data.** Equity comp is sensitive. Entitlement, aggregation thresholds, and PII handling are not afterthoughts.
- **AI-assisted coding is now the default.** Agents write a lot of our code. An agent with a vague prompt produces confident, plausible, wrong code — fast.

> Note: Land this: our biggest risk is not "can we write Spring Boot?" It is "do the six of us mean the same thing by *vested but unexercised*?"

---

## The failure mode we want to kill

**Vibe coding:** prompt → code → eyeball it → prompt again. The requirement lives in chat history and in someone's head.

Consequences we have all lived through:

| Symptom | Root cause |
|---|---|
| Two devs implement the same metric two different ways | Requirement never written down in one place |
| "That's not what I asked for" in demo | Intent existed only in a Slack thread |
| Agent refactors something unrelated | No stated boundaries or non-goals |
| Reviewer can't tell if the PR is *correct*, only whether it *compiles* | No acceptance criteria to review against |
| Onboarding a new dev takes weeks | Knowledge is in commit archaeology |

> Note: Ask the room for a show of hands on row 4. Everyone raises a hand.

---

## What Spec-Driven Development is

**Core inversion:** the specification is the primary artifact. Code is a *generated, replaceable* output of it.

Traditional flow:

```
idea → code → (docs, if we get to it)
```

Spec-driven flow:

```
idea → constitution (rules) → spec (what/why) → plan (how) → tasks (steps) → code (output)
        ↑____________________ each layer reviewed before the next ____________________↑
```

Three principles:

1. **Separate *what* from *how*.** The spec never names Spring Boot, Snowflake, or React. The plan does.
2. **Ambiguity is a bug, and it is cheapest to fix upstream.** A wrong word in a spec costs a sentence. The same wrong word in merged code costs a sprint.
3. **Every artifact is executable context.** The spec is not documentation for humans only — it is the input the agent compiles against.

> Note: The one-liner: "We are not writing docs. We are writing the source code of the source code."

---

## Why this matters more with AI agents

An agent is a very fast junior engineer with perfect recall and zero context.

- Give it a vague prompt → it fills gaps with invention, and the invention *looks* right.
- Give it a spec with acceptance criteria → the gaps are already closed, and you can mechanically check the output.

Specs also solve the **context window problem**. Instead of a 200-message chat thread, the agent reads `spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, and `constitution.md` — a compact, versioned, reviewable representation of everything decided so far. A new session (or a new teammate) starts with full context.

And critically: **specs are reviewable in a PR.** Diffing a spec change is how we catch a wrong business rule before a single line of Java exists.

---

## Benefits of SDD for WorkplaceIQ — business (split across 2 slides)

**1. The unified model gets designed before it gets coded.**
Our hardest problem is semantic, not technical. SDD forces the Shareworks-vs-E*TRADE mapping into a written, reviewed artifact — with named edge cases — before anyone writes an entity class.

**2. Product, data, and engineering argue on the spec, not on the PR.**
A spec is readable by a product manager and by a Snowflake data engineer. Both can object *cheaply*. Today those objections arrive at demo time.

**3. Metric definitions become auditable.**
When an admin asks "how is *participation rate* calculated?", the answer is a versioned requirement with an ID, not a SQL file. For equity comp data going to plan sponsors, that provenance matters.

**4. Compliance and entitlement become first-class.**
Constitution rules like "no endpoint returns cross-company data" and "no cohort smaller than N is displayed" are checked on *every* feature by `/speckit.analyze`, not remembered by whoever happens to review.

---

## Benefits of SDD for WorkplaceIQ — engineering

**5. Onboarding drops from weeks to a day.**
`specs/` is the project's actual brain. New joiner reads the constitution plus three specs and can contribute.

**6. Parallel work without collisions.**
Once `plan.md` fixes the API contract, UI and service work proceed independently against the same contract. Front-end can mock from `contracts/` on day one.

**7. Rewrites become cheap.**
The spec outlives the implementation. If we decide the aggregation layer belongs in Snowflake views instead of Java, we regenerate against the same spec rather than re-deriving intent.

**8. Review shifts from style to substance.**
Reviewers check code against numbered acceptance criteria. "Does this satisfy FR-004?" is a far better review question than "is this idiomatic?"

**9. Tests are derivable, not invented.**
Acceptance scenarios written as Given/When/Then in the spec become the integration test suite almost mechanically.

**10. It is the right time.** Greenfield is the *only* moment where you get the full benefit for free — there is no legacy spec debt to backfill.

> Note: If someone says "this is just waterfall" — see the objections slide. Short answer: iterations are per-feature and hours long, not per-release and months long.

---

## What Spec Kit is

**GitHub Spec Kit** is an open-source toolkit that turns SDD from a discipline into a set of slash commands your coding agent runs.

- Installed via a CLI called `specify`
- Works with 30+ AI coding agents (Copilot, Claude Code, Cursor, Gemini CLI, Codex, Windsurf, and others) — `specify integration list` shows the catalog for your version
- Drops templates, prompts, and scripts into your repo; the agent does the work, the templates keep it honest
- Everything it produces is plain Markdown in git

```bash
# one-off run with uvx (no install)
uvx --from git+https://github.com/github/spec-kit.git specify init workplaceiq-ui

# initialize into an existing repo, targeting a specific agent
specify init . --integration copilot
specify init . --force --integration copilot     # merge into non-empty dir
```

> Note: Recommend we pick ONE integration for the team so prompts stay consistent. Whichever agent the majority already has licensed.

---

## What Spec Kit puts in the repo

```
workplaceiq/
├── .specify/
│   ├── memory/
│   │   └── constitution.md        ← project-wide, non-negotiable rules
│   ├── scripts/bash/              ← helper scripts the commands call
│   │   ├── create-new-feature.sh
│   │   ├── setup-plan.sh
│   │   └── check-prerequisites.sh
│   └── templates/
│       ├── spec-template.md
│       ├── plan-template.md
│       └── tasks-template.md
└── specs/
    └── 001-unified-equity-position-api/
        ├── spec.md                ← WHAT and WHY (no tech)
        ├── plan.md                ← HOW (stack, architecture, phases)
        ├── tasks.md               ← ordered, checkable work items
        ├── research.md            ← decisions + rationale
        ├── data-model.md          ← entities, fields, relationships
        ├── quickstart.md          ← how to run/validate the feature
        ├── checklists/            ← ux.md, api.md, security.md, performance.md
        └── contracts/
            └── api-spec.json      ← OpenAPI contract
```

Two things to notice: **one folder per feature, numbered**, and **the constitution lives outside features** because it applies to all of them.

---

## The workflow at a glance

| # | Command | Phase | Produces | Gate? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `/speckit.constitution` | Govern | `.specify/memory/constitution.md` | — |
| 2 | `/speckit.specify` | Define | `specs/NNN-*/spec.md` | — |
| 3 | `/speckit.clarify` | Sharpen | updated `spec.md` | ✅ quality gate |
| 4 | `/speckit.checklist` | Validate reqs | `checklists/*.md` | ✅ quality gate |
| 5 | `/speckit.plan` | Design | `plan.md`, `research.md`, `data-model.md`, `contracts/` | — |
| 6 | `/speckit.tasks` | Decompose | `tasks.md` | — |
| 7 | `/speckit.analyze` | Cross-check | read-only report | ✅ quality gate |
| 8 | `/speckit.implement` | Build | source code, tests | — |
| 9 | `/speckit.converge` | Reconcile | appends "Convergence" phase to `tasks.md` | ✅ exit gate |

Also useful: `/speckit.taskstoissues` converts `tasks.md` into GitHub issues for tracking.

**Rule of thumb:** run 1→9 in order. Skip the three quality gates only for throwaway spikes. For anything touching the unified model, entitlement, or a sponsor-visible metric — run all of them.

> Note: Emphasise this is a loop, not a waterfall. A converge finding sends you back to specify or plan for the *next* small iteration.

